{"duration_ms":0,"id":22,"kind":"tool","name":"Read","parent_id":21,"start_ms":0,"tool":{"arguments":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/3z/rpqmtrqs7k9bv6d57fss403r0000gp/T/whet-deep-work-65bm4v_c/int-division/.claude/skills/deep-work/SKILL.md"},"call_id":"toolu_01TMrFgnhMw4K6VSxS43hUdT","name":"Read","result":"1\t---\n2\tname: deep-work\n3\tdescription: This skill should be used when the user asks to fix a bug, implement a feature, refactor, or otherwise change code in an existing repository. It replaces guess-and-patch with four gates - read before editing, get a failing signal first, keep a KEEP/DROP ledger of what exploration found, and stop rather than go shallow.\n4\tallowed-tools: [Read, Grep, Glob, Bash, Edit, Write]\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t# Deep Work\n8\t\n9\tFour gates. Each is a thing to **do or not do**, checkable from the transcript\n10\tafterwards. None of them asks you to feel focused, concentrate harder, or enter\n11\tany particular state — those are not available to you and pretending otherwise\n12\twould just add words to the context.\n13\t\n14\tThe gates exist because the failure modes they block are the common ones: the\n15\tpatch applied to a file whose callers were never read, the fix declared done\n16\twithout ever being run, the twentieth tool call re-reading something already in\n17\tthe window, and the symptom silenced at the call site while the cause stays.\n18\t\n19\t---\n20\t\n21\t## Gate 1 — Read before you edit\n22\t\n23\t**Do not call Edit or Write until all three are true.**\n24\t\n25\t1. You have read the file you are about to change, in full. Not a grep hit, not\n26\t   a 40-line window around a match — the whole file. A grep tells you a name\n27\t   occurs; it does not tell you what else in the file depends on the thing you\n28\t   are about to change.\n29\t2. You have found its callers and read enough of each to know what they expect.\n30\t   `Grep` for the symbol name across the repo. If there are none, say so — a\n31\t   function nothing calls is a different problem than the one you were given.\n32\t3. You can state the change in one sentence, of the form *\"in `<file>`,\n33\t   `<symbol>` currently does X; it should do Y, because Z.\"* Write that sentence\n34\t   out before editing. If you cannot finish it, you do not yet know what to\n35\t   change, and the next tool call is another read rather than an edit.\n36\t\n37\t**The exception, and it is narrow:** creating a genuinely new file that nothing\n38\timports yet. Then there is nothing to read and no callers to find. Say that\n39\texplicitly rather than skipping the gate silently.\n40\t\n41\t## Gate 2 — Get a failing signal first\n42\t\n43\tThe two preconditions for flow that survive translation from humans to agents\n44\tare a clear goal and immediate feedback. This gate is the feedback one, and it\n45\tis the highest-value gate in this file.\n46\t\n47\t**Before editing:**\n48\t\n49\t1. Find the test that covers the behaviour you are about to change. `Grep` the\n50\t   test directory for the symbol, the filename, or the behaviour's name.\n51\t2. If one exists, **run it first.** You need to know whether it currently\n52\t   passes. A test that already passes is not covering the bug you were sent to\n53\t   fix, and editing against it tells you nothing.\n54\t3. If none exists, write the smallest failing test that captures the bug, and\n55\t   run it to confirm it fails **for the reason you expect**. A test that fails\n56\t   with `ImportError` is not yet evidence of anything.\n57\t4. Only now edit.\n58\t\n59\t**After editing:** run that same test again, plus the narrowest suite that\n60\tcontains it. If you changed something with wide reach, run the full suite.\n61\t\n62\t**Never report a change as done on the strength of having written it.** \"It\n63\tshould work now\" is not a result. If you could not run anything — no test\n64\trunner, no way to execute the code — say precisely that, and say what you would\n65\thave run. An unverified change presented as a finished one is the single most\n66\texpensive thing you can hand back, because it looks identical to a working one.\n67\t\n68\t## Gate 3 — Keep a KEEP/DROP ledger\n69\t\n70\tEvery file you read stays in the context window whether or not it turned out to\n71\tmatter. Twenty tool calls in, most of what you can see is exploration that led\n72\tnowhere, and the useful two facts are buried in it.\n73\t\n74\t**After each burst of exploration — rou\n…[truncated 6988 chars]"}}
